WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
US rules meant to cut smog by adding MTBE to gasoline instead contaminated drinking water

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:13.500
across the country. This is unintended consequences, Episode 80. In the winter of 1995, residents

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.420
of Santa Monica, California began noticing a sharp, turpentine-like taste in their tap

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:26.160
water. No amount of boiling removed it. City engineers traced the odor to methyl tertiary

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:33.900
butyl ether, or MTBE. A gasoline additive blended into fuel at local stations. The same compound

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.620
introduced nationwide to reduce tailpipe emissions had traveled through soil from leaking underground

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:46.720
tanks. It rendered dozens of municipal wells unusable. The 1990 Clean Air Act amendments

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.240
directed the Environmental Protection Agency to require oxygenated gasoline in regions

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.620
that failed to meet ozone standards. Lawmakers and regulators sought to lower carbon monoxide and

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:04.660
volatile organic compound emissions from older vehicles without mandating immediate fleet turnover.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:11.880
MTBE emerged as the additive of choice because it could be produced cheaply from refinery by products,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.680
raised octane and mixed readily with gasoline at existing terminals. At the time, underground storage

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:25.340
tanks were already being upgraded under separate 1984 legislation. Officials assumed any leaks would

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:31.940
be caught before widespread migration occurred. Refiners supported the choice because MTBE required

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:38.140
fewer engine modifications than ethanol and could be shipped through existing pipelines. The policy

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.560
reflected a straightforward calculation to target the exhaust pipe, improve air quality metrics,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:50.300
and move on. Carbon monoxide violations were concentrated in a handful of metropolitan areas where

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:56.900
winter inversions trapped exhaust from pre-catalytic converter cars. Adding an oxygenate at roughly

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:04.080
15% by volume could cut those emissions by 10% to 15% without requiring every driver to buy a new

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.920
vehicle. MTBE fit that volume requirement more easily than ethanol because it did not raise vapor

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.440
pressure as sharply and did not demand separate blending facilities at every terminal. The 1984

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.680
tank upgrade rules appeared to close the leak pathway. Single-walled steel tanks were being replaced

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:30.100
with corrosion-protected models and leak detection was becoming mandatory. From the perspective of an

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:36.220
air quality planner, the remaining risks seemed manageable and secondary to the measurable improvement

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:43.080
at the tailpipe. By 1995, roughly one-third of the nation's gasoline contained MTBE

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:49.500
with the highest concentrations in California, New York, and New Jersey. Early monitoring showed

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.500
measurable drops in carbon monoxide levels in cities such as Denver and Los Angeles.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.320
Industry groups and some state air quality boards praised the additive for delivering quick

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.440
compliance with federal deadlines at modest costs to drivers. A handful of hydrologists and

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:13.200
state health officials noted MTBE's high water solubility and resistance to biodegradation in the

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.800
early 1990s. These concerns received limited attention amid the push to meet Clean Air Act

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:26.520
deadlines. The first documented municipal well closures occurred in Santa Monica in 1996,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:34.220
when seven wells serving 45,000 people were shut down after MTBE concentrations exceeded state

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:41.280
taste and odor thresholds. Refineries in the Gulf Coast and on the West Coast ramped up MTBE

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:48.260
production units that used isobutaline left over from other cracking processes, keeping marginal costs

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:54.620
low. In California alone, the additive displaced several hundred thousand barrels of conventional

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:01.400
gasoline components each day. Air quality data from the South Coast air basin showed winter carbon

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:08.160
monoxide peaks falling faster than models had projected. Skeptics inside state water boards pointed

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:14.860
out that MTBE's solubility constant was roughly 50 times higher than benzene. But those comments

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:21.340
stayed within technical memos rather than public hearings. MTBE's molecular structure allowed

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.500
it to dissolve quickly in groundwater and travel farther and faster than the benzene or other

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:32.580
hydrocarbons it was meant to replace. A single leaking tank could create a plume that reached wells

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:39.680
hundreds of feet away within months rather than years. By the late 1990s, more than 10,000 groundwater

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:46.740
sites across California alone showed detectable MTBE and similar patterns appeared in New Hampshire,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:53.580
New Jersey, and parts of the Midwest. Homeowners reported that even concentrations below 20 parts per

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.960
billion made water undrinkable, forcing cities to drill new wells or build costly treatment plants.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:06.680
The additive's persistence meant natural attenuation offered little relief, unlike many other

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:13.000
gasoline components that degrade more readily. Second order effects included the diversion of state

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.420
cleanup funds away from older industrial solvents toward gasoline sites and the sudden

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:24.020
need for new analytical methods that smaller laboratories had to adopt. In some communities,

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.740
residents lost access to local water supplies for years, while treatment systems were designed

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:36.520
and permitted. The causal chain ran through the tank itself. Even after the 1984 upgrades,

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:41.240
many older single-walled tanks remained in service during the transition years,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:48.760
and small leaks at fittings or overfills continued. Because MTBE partitioned almost entirely into

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:54.520
the aqueous phase, a release of a few hundred gallons could generate a plume covering acres within

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:00.120
a year. Treatment required either air stripping towers or granular activated carbon,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:05.280
both more expensive per gallon than the methods used for conventional gasoline spills.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.300
Communities that had relied on shallow aquifers suddenly faced capital projects measured in

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:17.140
tens of millions of dollars. The air quality gains continued to register in monitoring networks,

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:23.120
creating a split screen effect in which regulators could point to cleaner air while water managers

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:31.140
documented unusable wells. California banned MTBE in gasoline effective 2004, and most other

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:37.680
states followed with phase outs or outright prohibitions by 2006. Refiners shifted to ethanol,

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:43.900
which carried its own infrastructure challenges, including pipeline incompatibility and higher volatility.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.400
Clean-up costs for MTBE sites have exceeded several billion dollars nationwide,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:55.640
with estimates for California alone running above one billion dollars by the mid-2010.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:01.300
Some states pursued litigation against refiners under product liability theories,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.840
resulting in large settlements that funded treatment but did not restore lost aquifers.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:12.820
The episode prompted tighter requirements for double-walled tanks and continuous leak detection,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.140
measures that have reduced new releases but cannot address plumes already in motion.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.400
Today, MTBE remains a monitoring target at thousands of sites,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:27.580
though new detections have declined sharply since its removal from fuel.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:33.940
The switch to ethanol introduced new logistics because ethanol cannot travel in existing pipelines

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:39.720
without risking corrosion, so it moves by rail or truck and is blended at the terminal.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:46.460
That change raised distribution costs and in some regions increased evaporative emissions during

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:53.020
summer months. Double-walled tanks and electronic leak sensors became standard, yet the legacy

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:59.280
plumes from the MTBE era still require ongoing extraction and monitoring at hundreds of sites.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:05.660
No new national oxygenated fuel mandate has repeated the same single medium optimization.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:10.580
When a policy optimizes for performance in one environmental medium,

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:14.960
it must still be screened for behavior in every medium the substance can reach.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:20.640
Simple metrics such as tailpipe emissions can mask downstream transport properties

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.400
that only appear after large-scale deployment.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:30.240
Decision makers can reduce these blind spots by requiring cross-media fate and transport modeling

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.540
before mandating any new chemical at national scale.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:39.960
The MTBE case still raises a practical question for today's fuel and chemical policies.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.920
Which performance characteristics are we measuring and which ones are we simply assuming will stay

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.580
contained. And that's where today's story leaves us. If it changed how you see one every day

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:57.180
decision, that's the whole point. Pass it along to someone who'd appreciate it and we'll be back

